            <ArticleTitle>A 2:1 co-crystal of 3,5-di-bromo-4-cyano-benzoic acid and anthracene.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1743-1746</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1107/S2056989017014815</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The title co-crystal, C8H3Br2NO2·0.5C14H10, was self-assembled from a 2:1 mixture of the components in slowly evaporating di-chloro-methane. The mol-ecules adopt a sheet structure parallel to (1-12) in which carb-oxy hydrogen-bonded dimers and anthracene mol-ecules stagger in both dimensions. Within the sheets, six individual cyano acid mol-ecules surround each anthracene mol-ecule. Cyano acid mol-ecules form one of the two possible R22(10) rings between neighboring cyano and bromo groups. Compared to the di-chloro analog [Britton (2012 ▸). J. Chem. Crystallogr.42, 851-855], the dihedral angle between the best-fit planes of acid and anthracene mol-ecules has decreased from 7.1 to 0.9 (2)°.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
